  <!-- 1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY & PROBLEM STATEMENT                                 -->
  <!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <h1>1. Executive Summary & Problem Statement</h1>
  
  <p>
    Modern cloud architectures rely heavily on database persistence tiers such as PostgreSQL and Supabase. While these databases offer rich relational capabilities, queries under concurrent e-commerce workloads introduce high latencies (150ms - 220ms) and substantial operational egress and CPU costs ($0.00018 to $0.00030 per read).
  </p>

  <p>
    Traditional caching solutions rely on classical eviction algorithms—namely <strong>Least Recently Used (LRU)</strong> and <strong>Least Frequently Used (LFU)</strong>. While simple to implement, classical caching fails in real-world commercial production due to three critical architectural flaws:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Cost Blindness:</strong> Classical LRU treats all cache keys as computationally equivalent. In reality, evicting a 210ms generative AI recommendation query costs 25x more in compute and cloud egress than evicting a 2ms flat table lookup.</li>
    <li><strong>Cache Pollution (Scan Vulnerability):</strong> When automated web crawlers, inventory indexing scripts, or search bots issue sequential table scans, classical LRU blindly admits these single-use records into memory. Consequently, thousands of hot, revenue-generating commercial products are evicted from RAM, triggering immediate latency spikes.</li>
    <li><strong>Rigid Static Expiration (Blind TTL):</strong> Traditional caches assign fixed expiration windows (e.g., 60 seconds) irrespective of real-time velocity surges, leading to premature expirations during flash-sale events.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout-box">
    <div class="callout-title">Core Mission of PredictiveCache AI</div>
    To replace blind recency replacement with an intelligent <strong>Cost-Aware Predictive Utility Model</strong> that leverages machine learning to dynamically manage cache admission, proactive prefetching, and adaptive TTL extensions, delivering sub-12ms latency while reducing cloud database read costs by over 70%.
  </div>

  <!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <!-- 2. MATHEMATICAL FOUNDATION & COST-AWARE FORMULATION                       -->
  <!-- ========================================================================= -->
  <h1>2. Mathematical Formulation: Economic Utility U(k)</h1>
  
  <p>
    PredictiveCache AI evaluates every incoming request against a mathematical utility function $\mathcal{U}(k)$, balancing the expected economic value of caching against the physical opportunity cost of RAM reservation:
  </p>

  <div class="code-block">
Utility Function:
  U(k) = [ P(re-access) × Cost_fetch(k) ] - [ Cost_memory × Size(k) ] - LatencyPenalty

Where:
  • P(re-access)       : Predicted probability of re-access within time window T (0.0 to 1.0)
  • Cost_fetch(k)      : Actual financial + compute cost of database roundtrip ($/query)
  • Cost_memory        : Physical RAM allocation cost per Kilobyte ($/KB/sec)
  • Size(k)            : Memory footprint of the serialized JSON payload (KB)
  • LatencyPenalty     : Normalized latency SLA violation factor
  </div>

  <h3>Admission and Eviction Decision Logic:</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Positive Utility ($\mathcal{U}(k) > 0$):</strong> The resource provides positive net financial savings. It is admitted into <strong>APTS Cache</strong>, assigned an extended dynamic TTL (up to 300s), and flagged for proactive background re-warming.</li>
    <li><strong>Negative Utility ($\mathcal{U}(k) \le 0$):</strong> Cold queries, bot scans, or oversized low-reuse payloads are rejected from cache admission. They are served directly from the database through an ephemeral bypass, protecting high-utility commercial items in RAM.</li>
  </ul>

